// Package sys is the gogo system API: the wall clock, randomness, the
// platform configuration store and the program's arguments. It is a stub package like gogo/actor: the
// compiler replaces every call with a VM builtin (B_UNIX_TIME,
// B_SET_UNIX_TIME, B_RANDOM, B_CONFIG_GET), so the bodies are never
// executed. The C side is vm_unix_time_ms, vm_set_unix_time_ms, vm_random
// and vm_config_get (runtime/vm/host.h), over the host's optional RTC,
// random and config hooks. Args has no builtin: the compiler expands it into
// a lookup of the config key "\x00args" and a split (see Args).
//
// The wall clock is separate from actor.Now (milliseconds since the VM
// started): it can be unknown. gogo/sntp sets it from the network; a TLS
// client needs it to check certificate validity.
//
// Trust. The wall clock is trusted image state: gogo has no privilege model
// (every image and module already has the file system and the network), so
// any code may call SetTime, and SNTP is unauthenticated. The only
// protection is a floor: the time the firmware or VM was built. SetTime
// refuses an earlier time, so no one can move the clock back before the
// build and revive a certificate that expired before it. Certificate
// validity is protected by that floor and nothing else.
package sys

// Time returns the Unix time in milliseconds (UTC), or 0 if it is not known:
// nothing set it yet and the platform has no clock of its own. Once known it
// keeps advancing by itself.
func Time() int64 { panic("gogo only") }

// SetTime makes ms (Unix time, milliseconds) the current time from now on
// and returns true. It returns false and changes nothing if ms is earlier
// than the build-time floor (see above). ms <= 0 forgets the time (Time
// returns 0 again) and returns true.
func SetTime(ms int64) bool { panic("gogo only") }

// Random returns 30 random bits (0 <= n < 1<<30) from the platform's entropy
// source (the hardware generator on the board, getentropy on unix). It is
// meant for protocol nonces, not as a key generator on its own.
func Random() int { panic("gogo only") }

// Args returns the program's arguments: what follows the program on the
// command line of `gogo run prog a b` (or of a #! script, `./script a b`),
// without the program itself, so Args()[0] is "a". Unlike Go's os.Args there
// is no element for the program's name. It returns an empty, non-nil slice
// when there are none, and always on a board, where nothing starts a
// program with arguments. Every call returns a new slice, so changing it
// does not change what the next call returns. (The compiler expands the
// call inline over sys.Config, so it needs no VM support and no newer image
// format.)
func Args() []string { panic("gogo only") }

// Config returns the value of key in the platform configuration store; ok
// is false if key is not set, or starts with "wifi.". The store holds
// string keys and values, set with `gogo config set key value`: on unix
// the file $GOGO_CONFIG (default ~/.gogo/config), on the board its NVS.
// A key is 1 to 15 bytes of a-z, 0-9, "." and "_" (15 is the NVS limit).
// The prefixes "wifi.", "net.", "fs.", "code." and "gogo." are gogo's own
// (`gogo config keys` lists its keys, and `gogo config set` checks their
// values); other keys are the program's. Keys starting with "wifi." (Wi-Fi
// credentials) belong to the platform and are never visible to programs.
func Config(key string) (value string, ok bool) { panic("gogo only") }
